DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The tthhrr__kkiillll() and tthhrr__kkiillll22() system calls allow sending a signal, spec‐
     ified by the _s_i_g argument, to some threads in a process.  For the
     tthhrr__kkiillll() function, signalled threads are always limited to the current
     process.  For the tthhrr__kkiillll22() function, the _p_i_d argument specifies the
     process with threads to be signalled.

     The _i_d argument specifies which threads get the signal.  If _i_d is equal
     to -1, all threads in the specified process are signalled.  Otherwise,
     only the thread with the thread identifier equal to the argument is sig‐
     nalled.

     The _s_i_g argument defines the delivered signal.  It must be a valid signal
     number or zero.  In the latter case no signal is actually sent, and the
     call is used to verify the liveness of the thread.

     The signal is delivered with siginfo si_code set to SI_LWP.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     If successful, tthhrr__kkiillll() and tthhrr__kkiillll22() will return zero, otherwise -1
     is returned, and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The tthhrr__kkiillll() and tthhrr__kkiillll22() operations return the following errors:

     [EINVAL]           The _s_i_g argument is not zero and does not specify
                        valid signal.

     [ESRCH]            The specified process or thread was not found.

     Additionally, the tthhrr__kkiillll22() may return the following errors:

     [EPERM]            The current process does not have sufficient privilege
                        to check existence or send a signal to the specified
                        process.

